                        "review": "The game started okay-ish, but quickly became repetitive and badly paced.\n\nYou rapidly realize that, as a roguelite, you're supposed to progressively get stronger to make the game more manageable.\n\nBut as I kept playing, it stopped feeling like progression and started feeling like tedious grinding.\n\nI finally beat the first big area and started the second one, only to get absolutely stomped by random mobs. It felt like starting the game all over again. And it meant I still had a lot of grinding left ahead.\n\nI had already noticed some balancing issues earlier on, but in this new area, it's no longer just a balancing issue: playing some characters feels like trying to play with your feet.\n\nKevin, most notably, became unplayable when enemies got faster, stronger, and more aggressive. Kevin can only perform melee attacks with a tiny range, and his ability throws a few knives around that don't even hit all the enemies nearby. You're supposed to run into packs of mobs and elites, maybe land one little attack, and then roll away, or you'll die in 3 seconds flat.\n\nMeanwhile, Lucy can just spam fireballs from a safe distance and throw tornadoes that immobilize entire packs of enemies.\n\nAnd since the game encourages you to level up all the characters because some abilities are shared between them once unlocked, having to level up Kevin is a complete torture.\nIn the second biome, I died within 10 seconds with him.\n\nAnd I know I would most likely have beaten the game if I had a little more patience, but grinding isn't the only issue.\n\nThe storytelling is my other main issue.\n\nThe game decided that no character other than the narrator (who does have a nice voice) would speak. Why not? But personally, I think voices are very important when it comes to storytelling and immersion.\n\nThe main issue, though, is that even after 10 hours, all I know is that some sort of corruption is spreading throughout the world and that we need to stop it. This is literally still the premise of the game, and I still don't know much more than that.\n\nIt's also an issue because the story talks about a world being corrupted, but we never got to know that world before the corruption started spreading. We're immediately thrown into dungeons without ever getting the opportunity to understand what kind of world we're supposed to be saving.\n\nHonestly, it's not motivating me to keep playing.\n\nI just want to say that the game isn't bad per se. It's just not engaging enough to make me want to spend the little free time I already have on it.\n\nThe gameplay, despite its balancing issues and repetitiveness, isn't actually bad. I even enjoyed it a little at first. The art direction isn't bad either. Although, to be honest, it's not particularly impressive. The first biome was basically just cave after cave, all looking the same.",
